  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    Established at ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, the deep-tunnel Nightshelf colony, during the Clever Ice Age — specifically in response to orca raids that demonstrated the lethal cost of unsecured information. The Cell, the Nightshelf's owl-sworn enforcement network, codified the rule to govern the movement of three artifacts: ☽'visois' Mask, the grimoire ☽'numu' Witness, and the blade Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~. Routes went unspoken. Custodians' names went unwritten.
    
    The principle is absolute silence regarding artifact custody and certain trade operations. The Pactum subsumes relevant provisions of the Lex∴andatum, the Nightshelf's unified legal code, giving it precedence. Violation is not punished through judicial process — violators cease to be found in the tunnels. The archives record little about enforcement. The archives record little about the Pactum at all, which is the point.
    
    Each transaction is authenticated by the Swaleed~ Luminary in light-code legible only to the initiated. The Luminary's destruction would void the commercial charter the Pactum underpins — the continued legal force of a silence law thus depends on a single glass vessel scarred by three wars. This is either deliberate fragility or an oversight no one has survived long enough to correct.
    
    What began as wartime necessity has calcified into political infrastructure. The Fire Keepers and the Shadow Parliament use the Pactum to move resources beyond Aurora Stack oversight. It constrains inquiry as effectively as it constrains speech.
    
    For those most deeply bound, the constraint is not metaphorical. Bearers report a literal inability to speak — words dying before the beak can shape them, even when silence costs lives. The account from Augur's fire-core delivery through collapsing tunnels confirms this: the oath held even as the ice did not. Whether this is a property of the Pactum itself or of the artifacts it governs is a question no one bound by it can answer, for obvious reasons.
